---
import ProductionLayout from '../../layouts/ProductionLayout.astro';
import FinlayConcept from '../../missions/FinlayConcept';
import FinlayWorkbench from '../../missions/FinlayWorkbench';
import { FINLAY_ORBIT } from '../../missions/finlay-model';
---
<ProductionLayout title="15P/Finlay — A comet as a water depot · Skyhook Industries" description="Explore a mission to enclose comet 15P/Finlay, use its water for propulsion and establish an orbital depot. Compare plane-change, power and propellant assumptions.">
  <div class="finlay-page">
    <header class="finlay-hero">
      <p class="mission-eyebrow">MISSION STUDY / 15P FINLAY</p>
      <div class="finlay-hero-copy"><h1>A comet.<br/><em>A water depot.</em></h1><div><p>Enclose the nucleus. Use part of its water to move the rest. Keep that resource in an orbit where spacecraft can reach it.</p><a class="text-link" href="#mission-budget">Shape the mission <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div></div>
      <div class="finlay-target"><span><b>1.8 km</b>Adopted nucleus diameter</span><span><b>{FINLAY_ORBIT.inclinationDeg.toFixed(2)}°</b>Reference orbital inclination</span><span><b>{(FINLAY_ORBIT.periodDays/365.25).toFixed(2)} years</b>Reference orbit around the Sun</span></div>
      <p class="finlay-target-note">The diameter follows an adopted published model. Orbit values come from a frozen JPL solution; they do not describe a planned encounter.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="finlay-concept-section" aria-label="Finlay mission architecture"><FinlayConcept client:visible /><noscript><p>The enclosure concept is shown above. Enable JavaScript to explore the five mission stages.</p></noscript></section>
    <section class="finlay-intent">
      <p class="mission-eyebrow">THE MISSION</p>
      <div><h2>Move the resource.<br/>Build the network around it.</h2><p>The ambition is to redirect the whole nucleus of 15P/Finlay and establish a water depot near Earth, the Moon or Mars. A flexible enclosure collects released volatiles. A load-bearing harness secures the nucleus and carries the engines. Local heat, vapor handling and controlled thrust turn some of that water into the means of moving the rest.</p><p>Water could support propulsion, life support and industry without lifting every shipment from a planetary surface. Its usefulness depends on the depot’s actual orbit and the cost of reaching it.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="finlay-study" id="mission-budget" aria-labelledby="finlay-budget-heading">
      <div class="finlay-section-heading"><p class="mission-eyebrow">MISSION WORKBENCH / MASS + ENERGY</p><h2 id="finlay-budget-heading">Spend some water.<br/>Keep the rest.</h2><p>Try the two plane-change locations, then change power, exhaust speed and the water allocation. The results follow your assumptions; they are a first sizing exercise for the whole nucleus.</p></div>
      <FinlayWorkbench client:visible /><noscript><p>The default study is shown above. Enable JavaScript to change assumptions and compare the two plane-change locations.</p></noscript>
    </section>
    <section class="finlay-destinations">
      <div><p class="mission-eyebrow">THE DESTINATION</p><h2>An orbit that<br/>earns its keep.</h2><p>Plane alignment is one part of redirection. Each destination also needs a timed encounter, arrival-energy reduction and a stable depot orbit.</p></div>
      <div class="finlay-destination-list">
        <article><span>01 / EARTH</span><h3>Support departures from Earth.</h3><p>A high Earth orbit could serve departing spacecraft. Study access from the transport network, lunar perturbations and the long-term orbit.</p></article>
        <article><span>02 / MOON</span><h3>Supply the cislunar network.</h3><p>A distant lunar orbit is a candidate for storage and servicing. APIS studied delivering harvested water to a lunar distant retrograde orbit; retaining the whole Finlay nucleus there is a separate problem.</p></article>
        <article><span>03 / MARS</span><h3>Feed the Phobos hub.</h3><p>An accessible Mars-system depot could supply the Phobos network. Arrival geometry, capture energy and continuing orbit maintenance determine its value.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="finlay-evidence" id="research">
      <div class="finlay-section-heading"><p class="mission-eyebrow">RESEARCH + ENGINEERING</p><h2>Build from the work<br/>that already exists.</h2><p>The architecture connects real observations, prior mission studies and ground demonstrations. Enclosing and moving Finlay combines them at a much larger scale.</p></div>
      <div class="finlay-source-list">
        <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/general/apis-asteroid-provided-in-situ-supplies-100mt-of-water-from-a-single-falcon-9/"><span>NASA NIAC / MISSION CONCEPT</span><h3>APIS: enclose, extract and return water <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></h3><p>A proposed bag and solar-thermal system would mine a small asteroid and return about 100 tonnes of harvested water to lunar orbit.</p></a>
        <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20190027057"><span>NASA + HONEYBEE / GROUND DEMONSTRATION</span><h3>WINE: extraction through steam thrust <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></h3><p>A prototype extracted water from regolith simulant, collected it in a cold trap, transferred it to a tank and produced steam thrust in a vacuum chamber.</p></a>
        <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20130010670"><span>ASTEROID RETRIEVAL / MISSION STUDY</span><h3>Capturing a small body <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></h3><p>A NASA-hosted study considered returning an approximately seven-metre asteroid. Finlay’s adopted diameter is about 1,800 metres; bag structure, restraint and thrust transfer need their own development program.</p></a>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.06645"><span>YE ET AL. / OBSERVATIONAL STUDY</span><h3>The changing activity of 15P/Finlay <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></h3><p>The 2014–2015 outbursts matter for containment and operations. The paper’s model adopts a 0.9 km radius and assumes a bulk density of 500 kg/m³.</p></a>
      </div>
      <details class="finlay-method"><summary>Engineering questions and calculation provenance</summary>
        <h3>Start with reconnaissance.</h3><p>Measure the nucleus’s shape, spin, mass, cohesion, accessible ice and natural activity. The envelope must manage dust, leaks and gas pressure; its separate restraint structure must distribute thrust without relying on a loose surface to carry concentrated engine loads.</p>
        <h3>Collect vapor; condition the propellant.</h3><p>Heating ice in vacuum can release vapor directly by sublimation. The concept uses local heating, filtration, cold trapping and a controlled feed system; liquid water, where useful, would be handled in pressurized equipment. It does not require melting the entire nucleus inside the bag.</p>
        <h3>Close the power and trajectory budgets together.</h3><p>Power generation, storage, heat rejection, extraction losses, nozzle performance and engine lifetime need sizing. Distributed thrust and spin control must account for changing mass distribution and natural outgassing. A propagated transfer must include planetary perturbations, realistic finite thrust and the selected depot orbit.</p>
        <h3>Frozen inputs, reproducible results.</h3><p>The orbit is JPL SBDB solution {FINLAY_ORBIT.solutionId}, dated {FINLAY_ORBIT.solutionDate}, at epoch JD {FINLAY_ORBIT.epochJD} TDB in the J2000 ecliptic frame. It was retrieved on 8 October 2026. The workbench is a two-body screening calculation, not a current ephemeris or an optimized mission.</p><p><a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html#/?sstr=15P">Inspect the JPL record ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/RelyOnFire/skyhook-industries/blob/dribbble-visual-redesign/docs/missions/finlay-sbdb-2026-10-08.json">Frozen source snapshot ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/RelyOnFire/skyhook-industries/blob/dribbble-visual-redesign/docs/missions/finlay.md">Model, equations and assumptions ↗</a></p>
      </details>
    </section>
    <section class="finlay-next">
      <div><p class="mission-eyebrow">ONE CONNECTED EXPERIENCE</p><h2>Transport. Power.<br/>Water. Industry.</h2><p>Flight Studio explores momentum exchange. Expeditions lets you build the transport and supply network, including Ceres water returns and Phobos processing. Finlay gives that growing infrastructure a longer-term mission.</p><p class="finlay-next-note">Finlay is a mission study. The playable campaign currently supplies water from Ceres.</p></div>
      <nav aria-label="Continue exploring"><a href="/lab/campaign/"><span>PLAY</span><strong>Build the supply chain</strong><small>Open Expeditions ↗</small></a><a href="/lab/"><span>EXPERIMENT</span><strong>Explore momentum exchange</strong><small>Open Flight Studio ↗</small></a><a href="/roadmap/#finlay-study"><span>DEVELOP</span><strong>Follow the mission questions</strong><small>Read the roadmap ↗</small></a></nav>
    </section>
  </div>
</ProductionLayout>
<style>
  .finlay-page{max-width:1240px;margin:auto;padding:0 4vw;color:#dce6e9}.mission-eyebrow{font:11px/1.5 ui-monospace,monospace;letter-spacing:.13em;color:#bb8d68;margin:0 0 24px}.finlay-hero{padding:70px 0 36px}.finlay-hero-copy{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr;gap:60px;align-items:end}.finlay-hero h1{font-size:clamp(44px,6.5vw,78px);font-weight:450;line-height:1.05;letter-spacing:-.045em;margin:0}.finlay-hero em{font-style:normal;color:#b6cad3}.finlay-hero-copy p{color:#afc0c9;font-size:18px;line-height:1.7;max-width:43ch;margin:0 0 23px}.finlay-target{display:flex;gap:40px;border-top:1px solid #2b3a42;margin-top:44px;padding-top:22px}.finlay-target span{font-size:10px;color:#8ca3af}.finlay-target b{display:block;color:#d6e1e6;font-size:21px;font-weight:450;margin-bottom:4px;white-space:nowrap}.finlay-target-note{font-size:10px;line-height:1.6;color:#7893a3;margin:13px 0 0;max-width:88ch}.finlay-concept-section{margin:0 0 70px}
  .finlay-intent{display:grid;grid-template-columns:200px 1fr;gap:42px;padding:0 0 65px;border-bottom:1px solid #293a44}.finlay-page h2{font-size:clamp(28px,3.6vw,44px);font-weight:450;line-height:1.15;letter-spacing:-.03em;margin:0 0 24px}.finlay-intent p:not(.mission-eyebrow){color:#a0b6c2;max-width:73ch;font-size:15px;line-height:1.85;margin:14px 0 0}.finlay-study{padding:70px 0;scroll-margin-top:25px}.finlay-section-heading{max-width:760px;margin-bottom:32px}.finlay-section-heading>p:last-child{color:#9eb4c1;font-size:15px;line-height:1.8;max-width:70ch}.finlay-page a:focus-visible{outline:2px solid #efb487;outline-offset:5px}
  .finlay-destinations{display:grid;grid-template-columns:.8fr 1.2fr;gap:60px;padding:60px 0;border-top:1px solid #293a44;border-bottom:1px solid #293a44}.finlay-destinations>div>p:last-child{font-size:14px;color:#94acb9;line-height:1.8;max-width:37ch}.finlay-destination-list article{border-top:1px solid #293a44;padding:23px 0}.finlay-destination-list article:first-child{border-top:0;padding-top:0}.finlay-destination-list span{font:10px ui-monospace,monospace;letter-spacing:.12em;color:#b78b69}.finlay-destination-list h3{font-size:20px;font-weight:450;margin:10px 0}.finlay-destination-list p{font-size:13px;color:#94acb9;line-height:1.8;margin:0}
  .finlay-evidence{padding:70px 0}.finlay-source-list{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:0 40px}.finlay-source-list>a{padding:25px 0;border-top:1px solid #31414a;color:inherit;text-decoration:none}.finlay-source-list>a:hover h3{color:#efb383}.finlay-source-list span{font:9px/1.6 ui-monospace,monospace;letter-spacing:.09em;color:#b18b6c}.finlay-source-list h3{font-size:19px;line-height:1.4;font-weight:450;margin:12px 0}.finlay-source-list b{font-weight:400;color:#c5916a}.finlay-source-list p{font-size:12px;color:#90a8b5;line-height:1.75;margin:0}.finlay-method{border-block:1px solid #293a44;margin-top:20px;padding:0 0 12px}.finlay-method summary{padding:22px 0;font-size:14px;color:#ceae94;cursor:pointer}.finlay-method h3{font-size:17px;font-weight:450;margin:20px 0 10px}.finlay-method p{font-size:13px;color:#92aab8;line-height:1.8;max-width:92ch}.finlay-method a{color:#d4b08e}
  .finlay-next{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.15fr 1fr;gap:55px;padding:45px 0 75px;border-top:1px solid #293a44}.finlay-next p:not(.mission-eyebrow){font-size:14px;color:#9ab0bd;line-height:1.8;max-width:51ch}.finlay-next .finlay-next-note{font-size:11px!important;color:#849ca9!important}.finlay-next nav{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px;justify-content:center}.finlay-next nav a{display:block;padding:18px 22px;border:1px solid #34454f;border-radius:6px;text-decoration:none}.finlay-next nav a:hover{border-color:#a77f61;background:#e2aa7710}.finlay-next nav span{font:9px ui-monospace,monospace;letter-spacing:.15em;color:#af8869}.finlay-next nav strong{display:block;color:#dce6eb;font-size:17px;font-weight:450;margin:7px 0}.finlay-next nav small{font-size:11px;color:#9fb8c5}
  @media(max-width:760px){.finlay-hero{padding-top:45px}.finlay-hero-copy{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:26px}.finlay-hero-copy p{font-size:16px}.finlay-target{gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:28px}.finlay-intent,.finlay-destinations,.finlay-next{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:20px}.finlay-intent{padding-bottom:40px}.finlay-concept-section{margin-bottom:45px}.finlay-study,.finlay-evidence{padding:45px 0}.finlay-destinations{padding:40px 0}.finlay-next{padding-bottom:50px}.finlay-source-list{grid-template-columns:1fr}.finlay-page h2{font-size:32px}.mission-eyebrow{margin-bottom:18px}}
  @media(max-width:400px){.finlay-hero h1{font-size:44px}.finlay-target{gap:14px 24px}.finlay-target b{font-size:19px}.finlay-target span{font-size:9px}.finlay-page h2{font-size:29px}.finlay-next nav a{padding:16px}.mission-eyebrow{font-size:10px}}
</style>
